#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c1ce9a-bad7-50d6-b6c7-a1423ccce4bb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Geometric Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c85153c-eb9f-550b-b019-defd84334f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364279} {:answer/id #uuid "5b503cab-5b6a-55a8-a286-2202aa79873e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364280} {:answer/id #uuid "ebee5405-1c8c-5d37-a7e3-207dadaf3e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364281} {:answer/id #uuid "aa51c0ad-dc04-575e-af4e-4abd53549441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364282} {:answer/id #uuid "c45850c2-8228-56b4-ba02-1015d5d01010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b503cab-5b6a-55a8-a286-2202aa79873e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4de825f7-0447-53f5-b85a-6e0112744178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "181d1dda-5cff-574e-8975-8c6dc281393d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130486" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of files reviewed by an antivirus until the first infected file is found\n- The number of infected files found in a computer\n- The number of crayons randomly picked out from a case before a blue crayon is picked" :question/worked-solution "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Reviewing a file is a binomial experiment, and the number of files reviewed before the first infected file is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Finding an infected file is a binomial experiment, but the number of infected files in a computer cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Randomly picking out a crayon is a binomial experiment, and the number of crayons picked out before a blue crayon is picked can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
